    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1043">

                <pb facs="1222" n="1026" xml:id="pg1026"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of the Army Files: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1043<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Director of the Division of Ship
                        Requirements, War Shipping Administration</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bissell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to Mr. M. F. Millikan, of the
                        Division of Ship Requirements, War Shipping Administration</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-22">22 July 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Victory</hi> 204. To Conway for Millikan WSA
                    Washington from Bissell.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In preliminary discussion of coal problems Leathers has stated that U. K.
                        cannot export any coal whatever to any destination after August shipments
                        for September arrival excepting anthracite duffs for briquetting and special
                        grades required North Africa.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>He is most anxious to continue to obtain not less than 90,000 tons per
                        month of bunker coal from U. S. especially in view of assumption by U. K. of
                        responsibility for bunkers for captured and released shipping to extent of
                        about 70,000 tons per month.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Have advised Leathers that MWT might have to make cash purchases. He will
                        discuss financial arrangements with Clayton and Collado here.<anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d1043-f1"/></item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Request you discuss with Potter and advise urgently his views on
                        availability of coal from U. S. and reasonableness of British refusal to
                        continue exports.</item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>Bissell plans to go to London 23rd or 24th therefore reply should be to
                        London.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1043-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1043-f1-content">
                <p>No documentation on the substance of such a discussion has been found. For an
                    indication that Clayton did discuss the coal question with members of the
                    British Delegation, see <ref target="#d1049">document No. 1049</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
